00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:09.040
And then trick number three is called
tools. And of course this is this is a

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:14.559
really exciting one and it really took
off in a massive way last year and and a

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:21.439
bit the year before as well. tools is
about realizing that when we have LLMs

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:26.560
generate tokens, the most likely output,
we could use those tokens not just to

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:32.000
generate just a a text response, but
also to decide whether or not the LLM

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:37.040
would like to carry out some actions
that it's been told it could do. So in

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:41.840
the prompt in in the input that you send
the LLM, you can say, "Hey, I'd like you

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:46.559
to answer my question, but by the way,
you can also respond with some special

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:50.559
tokens to say, I would like to search
the internet or I would like to use a

00:00:48.559 --> 00:00:55.360
calculator or I would like to run some
Python code and then tell me what." And

00:00:53.039 --> 00:01:00.559
if you respond with that, then I will do
that action for you and then I will call

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:06.400
you a second time with the results. And
that little clever trick which is called

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:11.760
using tools, it allows us to use an LLM
not just to generate a text output but

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:16.000
actually to decide to do something. We
call it running a tool and then we will

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.799
run the tool and provide it back with
the response. But you always have to

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:26.880
stay grounded in the reality that the
LLM itself isn't going off and searching

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:32.640
the internet or running Python code. An
LLM is just a statistical engine that

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:36.320
generates tokens, but we are
interpreting those tokens. It's

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.320
generating tokens that says something
like, I would like to run a calculator.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.479
And we're interpreting that output and
we're running the calculator. So, it's

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:49.840
always our code. It's always software
that ends up calling a tool. We're using

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:56.320
the LLM just to generate the tokens to
say what it wants to do next. And that

00:01:52.399 --> 00:02:00.399
whole process is called tools. and it's
really clever. All right, I just want to

